1
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:05,220
How much time do we have?

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:07,390
Uh, T-minus five hours, 37 minutes

3
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:09,020
to onset of meteor shower.

4
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:13,120
Okay, our position is 34.48 degrees north, 118.31 west.

5
00:00:13,190 --> 00:00:15,690
That means the azimuth should be...

6
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:18,920
168.22 degrees relative to magnetic north

7
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:21,620
with an elevation of 49.93.

8
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:22,950
Anything yet?

9
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,850
Uh, we have a signal, but there's no frame lock.

10
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:26,990
Hang on. How about... now?

11
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:28,950
We did it.

12
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:32,390
We have the West Coast feed of HBO.

13
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:35,220
Ooh, Real Sex.

14
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:36,990
Big deal. Every time I watch that show,

15
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:39,290
it's old women putting condoms on cucumbers.

16
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:43,150
All right, let's see what's on the East Coast feed.

17
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:45,920
Oh, hey. Dune.

18
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:49,520
Not a great movie,

19
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:51,490
but look at that beautiful desert.

20
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:55,950
Too bad Sheldon couldn't come with us.

21
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:58,020
Yeah, it's just not the same without him.

22
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:08,250
Oh, this sucks. I'm switching back to Real Sex.

23
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:14,320
Sheldon's log.

24
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:18,120
Stardate 63345.3.

25
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:19,990
While my colleagues are off

26
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:21,790
observing the Leonid meteor shower,

27
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:24,450
I have remained behind to complete my paper

28
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:27,720
on the decays of highly excited massive string states.

29
00:01:27,790 --> 00:01:29,790
Although my research is going well,

30
00:01:29,850 --> 00:01:33,290
I do miss the warmth of human companionship.

31
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,930
And good evening to you, Siam Palace.

32
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,530
This is Sheldon Cooper.

33
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,700
Yeah, I'm going to be dining alone this evening,

34
00:02:11,770 --> 00:02:14,200
so I'll be reducing my usual order.

35
00:02:14,270 --> 00:02:17,070
I'd like to start with one quarter

36
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:18,800
of the Assorted Appetizers Plate

37
00:02:18,870 --> 00:02:22,830
and, uh, one half of the Golden Treasure for two.

38
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:26,200
Oh, for heaven's sake. In the mid-18th century,

39
00:02:26,270 --> 00:02:29,100
King Rama IV of Siam divided a huge empire

40
00:02:29,170 --> 00:02:31,000
amongst the colonial powers of Europe

41
00:02:31,070 --> 00:02:32,700
in order to preserve his throne.

42
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:34,670
Surely, you, his cultural descendant,

43
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:37,200
can handle pad Thai and dumplings.

44
00:02:37,270 --> 00:02:39,030
Sheldon, help!

45
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:41,500
I need to go-- but you keep in mind

46
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:42,970
that my sharply worded comments

47
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:45,100
on Yelp.com recently took down a local muffin store.

48
00:02:49,630 --> 00:02:52,100
Penny. Penny. Penny.

49
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:54,430
Come in! Hurry!

50
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,070
Penny?

51
00:02:57,130 --> 00:02:58,430
I'm back here.

52
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:05,900
Penny. Penny. Penny.

53
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:09,270
Oh, for God's sakes, I'm in the bathroom!

54
00:03:09,330 --> 00:03:12,630
Shall I come back at a better time?

55
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:15,230
Get in here!

56
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:17,130
Hurry!

57
00:03:18,530 --> 00:03:20,430
Don't you dare knock!

58
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:25,770
Hello.

59
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:26,870
I slipped in the shower,

60
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:28,600
and I think I dislocated my shoulder.

61
00:03:30,130 --> 00:03:31,400
Not surprising.

62
00:03:31,470 --> 00:03:33,530
You have no safety mat or adhesive stickers

63
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,500
to allow for purchase on a surface

64
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:38,130
with a low coefficient of static friction.

65
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:40,230
What?

66
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:42,630
Tubs are slippery.

67
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:44,930
I know. I slipped.

68
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,970
I have a series of whimsical duck stickers

69
00:03:47,030 --> 00:03:49,030
on the bottom of my tub.

70
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:50,170
Yeah, okay, whatever.

71
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:52,000
Will you just turn the water off and help me up?

72
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:54,970
They're holding umbrellas.

73
00:03:55,030 --> 00:03:56,700
What?

74
00:03:56,770 --> 00:03:58,230
The ducks in my tub.

75
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:02,330
They're whimsical because ducks have neither a need for,

76
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:04,700
nor the ability to use, umbrellas.

77
00:04:04,770 --> 00:04:07,130
Oh, my God. I got to go to the emergency room.

78
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:09,430
Well, assuming you're correct that your right humerus

79
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,100
is no longer seated in the glenoid socket,

80
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:13,770
- I would certainly think so.  - Okay, can you drive me?

81
00:04:13,830 --> 00:04:15,230
I don't drive.

82
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,370
Well, I can't drive!

83
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:19,230
Well, it seems we've reached an impasse.

84
00:04:20,630 --> 00:04:22,800
I could call you a cab or an ambulance.

85
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:24,470
No, no, no. I can't wait that long.

86
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:25,830
You got to help me, please.

87
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,870
All right. Let it never be said that Sheldon Lee Cooper

88
00:04:29,930 --> 00:04:31,970
ignored the pleas of a damsel in distress.

89
00:04:32,030 --> 00:04:33,800
No one's saying that. Let's go.

90
00:04:33,870 --> 00:04:35,530
Well, it does seem rather ironic

91
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,700
that for want of 99-cent adhesive ducks,

92
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:40,000
we both might die in a fiery car crash.

93
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:43,700
I wish Penny didn't have to work.

94
00:04:43,770 --> 00:04:44,830
She loves camping.

95
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:46,430
Yeah, that would have been great.

96
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:48,130
You and Penny having sex in the tent

97
00:04:48,230 --> 00:04:51,000
while I sit out here and watch Howard hump a cactus.

98
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:55,630
Okay, the best I can tell,

99
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:57,230
there are eight other campsites nearby.

100
00:04:57,300 --> 00:04:58,670
Mostly science nerds like us,

101
00:04:58,730 --> 00:05:01,800
but just over yon ridge are two

102
00:05:01,870 --> 00:05:03,700
not unattractive middle school teachers

103
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:05,500
who reek of desperation.

104
00:05:05,570 --> 00:05:07,470
Wow. Wonderful.

105
00:05:07,530 --> 00:05:09,200
- How old are they? - I don't know.

106
00:05:09,270 --> 00:05:10,870
50, 55.

107
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:13,770
Oh, menopause.

108
00:05:14,970 --> 00:05:17,300
Nature's birth control.

109
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:18,470
Come on.

110
00:05:18,530 --> 00:05:20,230
You guys can't be that hard up.

111
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:22,370
- I am. - Yeah, me, too.

112
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:25,730
Look-- they gave me homemade cookies.

113
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:26,770
Of course, they did.

114
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:28,430
That's what grandmothers do.

115
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:30,070
So, what are we waiting for?

116
00:05:30,130 --> 00:05:31,170
Relax.

117
00:05:31,230 --> 00:05:32,970
I said we'd stop by a little later

118
00:05:33,030 --> 00:05:35,100
after they have their nap.

119
00:05:35,170 --> 00:05:36,170
Good idea.

120
00:05:36,230 --> 00:05:37,330
They'll be refreshed.

121
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:38,770
Cookie?

122
00:05:38,830 --> 00:05:40,530
Yeah. Thank you.

123
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:45,530
Not bad.

124
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:46,970
Yeah, very tasty.

125
00:05:47,030 --> 00:05:48,700
Well, so tell me more about these teachers.

126
00:05:48,770 --> 00:05:50,000
Not much to tell.

127
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:51,730
They had a VW microbus

128
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:54,770
and were wearing tie-dyed Grateful Dead T-shirts.

129
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:00,930
Good cookies.

130
00:06:05,830 --> 00:06:09,730
I see no organizational system in here whatsoever.

131
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:13,430
Which panties do you wear on Mondays?

132
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,200
I don't need panties.

133
00:06:15,270 --> 00:06:16,900
I just need shorts and a shirt.

134
00:06:16,970 --> 00:06:18,200
My mother always told me

135
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:19,430
one should wear clean underpants

136
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,100
in case one is in an accident.

137
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:23,200
One was already in an accident.

138
00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:24,870
That doesn't mean one won't be in another,

139
00:06:24,930 --> 00:06:26,300
especially if I'm driving.

140
00:06:26,370 --> 00:06:27,500
Clothes, Sheldon.

141
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:28,900
- I need clothes! - Okay.

142
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:30,670
Here.

143
00:06:30,730 --> 00:06:32,430
Seriously? Those shorts with that top?

144
00:06:34,130 --> 00:06:36,100
All right.

145
00:06:37,370 --> 00:06:39,000
No.

146
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:42,900
No.

147
00:06:42,970 --> 00:06:44,670
Oh, that's cute.

148
00:06:45,870 --> 00:06:48,070
We should have you checked for a concussion.

149
00:06:48,130 --> 00:06:49,900
Okay.

150
00:06:49,970 --> 00:06:52,130
Now, you got to help me put these on.

151
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:54,130
- All right. - But don't look.

152
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:57,000
- Don't look? - I don't want you to see me naked.

153
00:06:57,070 --> 00:06:58,070
Oh.

154
00:06:58,130 --> 00:06:59,730
Well, that's understandable.

155
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,600
You may be interested to know

156
00:07:01,670 --> 00:07:03,170
that a prohibition against looking

157
00:07:03,230 --> 00:07:05,400
is well established in heroic mythology.

158
00:07:05,470 --> 00:07:08,600
Uh, Lot and his wife, Perseus and Medusa,

159
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:10,430
- Orpheus and Eurydice... - Yeah, great.

160
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:11,500
They always look.

161
00:07:11,570 --> 00:07:13,370
It never ends well.

162
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:15,970
Okay. Now you got help me get my arm into the sleeve.

163
00:07:16,030 --> 00:07:17,400
Okay.

164
00:07:21,030 --> 00:07:22,630
Is that my arm?

165
00:07:25,630 --> 00:07:28,670
It doesn't feel like an arm.

166
00:07:28,730 --> 00:07:31,570
Then, maybe you should let it go.

167
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:35,430
All righty.

168
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:50,500
Stars are pretty, aren't they?

169
00:07:52,130 --> 00:07:54,630
Up above the world so high.

170
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:58,200
Like little diamonds in the sky.

171
00:07:58,270 --> 00:08:01,530
That's beautiful, dude.

172
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:03,230
You should... you should write that down

173
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:05,070
before someone steals it.

174
00:08:05,130 --> 00:08:07,170
So, when do the meteors get here?

175
00:08:07,230 --> 00:08:08,900
The meteors don't get here.

176
00:08:08,970 --> 00:08:10,930
The Earth is moving into their path.

177
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:12,900
I can feel it.

178
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:18,300
I can feel the Earth moving.

179
00:08:19,330 --> 00:08:20,570
It's moving too fast.

180
00:08:20,630 --> 00:08:22,300
Raj, slow it down.

181
00:08:31,130 --> 00:08:33,330
Okay, how's that?

182
00:08:34,370 --> 00:08:35,600
Better. Thanks.

183
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:39,570
Stars are pretty, aren't they?

184
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,600
What's so funny?

185
00:08:45,670 --> 00:08:47,030
It's your American accent.

186
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:48,600
Everything you say sounds stupid.

187
00:08:51,130 --> 00:08:52,970
"Stars are pretty, aren't they?"

188
00:09:01,470 --> 00:09:04,330
For the record, I should let you know

189
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:08,000
this is my first time driving an actual motor vehicle.

190
00:09:09,300 --> 00:09:11,070
You have your learner's permit, right?

191
00:09:11,130 --> 00:09:12,500
Yes, and I have logged

192
00:09:12,570 --> 00:09:15,070
a considerable number of hours on a simulator.

193
00:09:15,130 --> 00:09:17,770
- Good. - Didn't work out well.

194
00:09:17,830 --> 00:09:19,770
- All right, can we please go? - One moment.

195
00:09:19,830 --> 00:09:21,730
According to my driver's ed book,

196
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:23,900
the side mirror is properly adjusted

197
00:09:23,970 --> 00:09:26,170
when the driver's door handle

198
00:09:26,230 --> 00:09:27,770
is visible in the lower right corner.

199
00:09:27,830 --> 00:09:28,870
There's the handle.

200
00:09:28,930 --> 00:09:30,270
Oh, no, too far.

201
00:09:30,330 --> 00:09:33,000
Bringing it back.

202
00:09:33,070 --> 00:09:34,370
Optimized.

203
00:09:34,430 --> 00:09:36,070
Now, where is the switch

204
00:09:36,130 --> 00:09:38,200
to adjust the passenger side mirror?

205
00:09:38,270 --> 00:09:40,130
It's right there.

206
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:43,970
Where is the passenger side mirror?

207
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,170
In a parking lot in Hollywood.

208
00:09:47,230 --> 00:09:49,430
- Now, can we please go? - Yes.

209
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,800
As soon as I adjust my seat.

210
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,100
Oh, dear.

211
00:10:02,170 --> 00:10:04,030
I'm going to have to start again.

212
00:10:12,930 --> 00:10:14,930
If I could speak the language of rabbits,

213
00:10:14,930 --> 00:10:18,830
that will be amazed, I will be their king.

214
00:10:20,230 --> 00:10:23,100
I hate my name.

215
00:10:24,970 --> 00:10:27,430
It has "nerd" in it.

216
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:33,500
Len-nerd.

217
00:10:33,570 --> 00:10:36,870
I lost my virginity to my cousin Jeanie.

218
00:10:39,300 --> 00:10:41,570
I would be kind to my rabbit subjects.

219
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:46,100
At first.

220
00:10:46,170 --> 00:10:48,830
You know what's a cool name?

221
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:51,370
Angelo.

222
00:10:51,430 --> 00:10:54,770
That has angel and Jell-O in it.

223
00:10:55,970 --> 00:10:57,900
It was my Uncle Murray's funeral.

224
00:10:57,970 --> 00:11:00,400
We were all back at my Aunt Barbara's house.

225
00:11:00,470 --> 00:11:02,870
Our eyes locked over the pickled herring.

226
00:11:02,930 --> 00:11:04,930
We never meant for it to happen.

227
00:11:06,430 --> 00:11:09,500
One day, I hold a great ball for the President of France,

228
00:11:09,570 --> 00:11:11,700
but the rabbits-- they hate me and don't come.

229
00:11:11,770 --> 00:11:14,670
I am embarrassed, so I eat all the lettuce in the world

230
00:11:14,730 --> 00:11:16,730
and make them watch.

231
00:11:18,030 --> 00:11:20,030
People could call me Angie.

232
00:11:22,230 --> 00:11:25,300
(la Sylvester Stallone): Yo, Angie, how's it goin'?

233
00:11:25,370 --> 00:11:28,470
To this day, I can't look at pickled herring

234
00:11:28,530 --> 00:11:31,530
without being aroused and ashamed.

235
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:35,130
Oh! Cousin Jeanie.

236
00:11:40,130 --> 00:11:42,830
Could you please drive a little faster?

237
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:45,630
Oh, I think we're going sufficiently fast.

238
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:48,600
What's that?

239
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:50,670
Nothing. The engine does that sometimes.

240
00:11:50,730 --> 00:11:51,830
It can't be nothing.

241
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:53,000
The check engine light is on.

242
00:11:53,070 --> 00:11:54,400
We need to find a service station.

243
00:11:54,470 --> 00:11:56,730
No. The light has been on since I bought the car.

244
00:11:56,800 --> 00:11:58,100
All the more reason

245
00:11:58,170 --> 00:12:00,900
to consult with a mechanic before it explodes.

246
00:12:00,970 --> 00:12:02,200
It's not going to explode.

247
00:12:02,270 --> 00:12:03,500
Just keep driving.

248
00:12:03,570 --> 00:12:06,770
Warp speed ahead, Mr. Spock.

249
00:12:06,830 --> 00:12:10,870
Mr. Spock did not pilot the Enterprise.

250
00:12:10,930 --> 00:12:13,600
He was the science officer, and I guarantee you that

251
00:12:13,670 --> 00:12:16,200
if he ever saw the Enterprise's check engine light blinking,

252
00:12:16,270 --> 00:12:19,000
he would pull the ship over immediately.

253
00:12:19,070 --> 00:12:22,430
Oh, God, I'm gonna lose the arm.

254
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:23,630
Oh, well, red light.

255
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:25,800
Release accelerator

256
00:12:25,870 --> 00:12:29,930
and slowly apply... the... brake.

257
00:12:32,430 --> 00:12:34,530
Nailed it.

258
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:40,630
While we have a moment, may I ask you a question?

259
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:42,030
What?

260
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:44,400
Why do you have the Chinese character for soup

261
00:12:44,470 --> 00:12:46,500
tattooed on your right buttock?

262
00:12:46,570 --> 00:12:48,200
It's not soup.

263
00:12:48,270 --> 00:12:49,500
It's courage.

264
00:12:49,570 --> 00:12:50,970
No, it isn't.

265
00:12:51,030 --> 00:12:53,200
But I suppose it does take courage

266
00:12:53,270 --> 00:12:57,030
to demonstrate that kind of commitment to soup.

267
00:12:57,100 --> 00:12:58,970
How did you see it?

268
00:12:59,030 --> 00:13:00,470
You said you wouldn't look.

269
00:13:00,530 --> 00:13:01,670
Sorry.

270
00:13:01,730 --> 00:13:04,930
As I told you, the hero always peeks.

271
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:15,460
Anything?

272
00:13:15,530 --> 00:13:18,030
No. That was the last pudding cup.

273
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,200
No!

274
00:13:20,260 --> 00:13:22,500
What about Slim Jims?

275
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:26,060
That's what he used to eat his pudding, remember?

276
00:13:26,130 --> 00:13:30,360
Right. That was so good!

277
00:13:30,430 --> 00:13:34,360
Sweety and meaty at the same time.

278
00:13:34,430 --> 00:13:36,660
Wait. Wait, wait. So you're saying

279
00:13:36,730 --> 00:13:38,060
we're out of food?

280
00:13:38,130 --> 00:13:40,700
The only thing in here are blue ice packs.

281
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:42,800
I know they're poison,

282
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:46,760
but they look like big, yummy otter pops.

283
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:49,900
Oh, God, I am so hungry.

284
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:51,100
Me, too.

285
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:54,660
Check and see if we have any more pudding.

286
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:58,800
Okay.

287
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:03,630
All right, there's no need to bark at me.

288
00:14:05,430 --> 00:14:06,770
According to the inexplicably irritable nurse

289
00:14:06,770 --> 00:14:07,730
behind the desk,

290
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:09,300
you'll be seen after the man

291
00:14:09,360 --> 00:14:11,400
who claims to be having a heart attack,

292
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:12,960
but appears to be well enough

293
00:14:13,030 --> 00:14:15,760
to play Doodle Jump on his iPhone.

294
00:14:15,830 --> 00:14:18,630
We have to fill these out.

295
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,430
"Describe illness or injury."

296
00:14:22,730 --> 00:14:24,500
I dislocated my shoulder.

297
00:14:24,500 --> 00:14:26,160
All right.

298
00:14:26,230 --> 00:14:28,930
And how did the accident occur?

299
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,400
You already know that.

300
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:33,460
"Cause of accident."

301
00:14:33,530 --> 00:14:35,630
"Lack of adhesive ducks."

302
00:14:37,630 --> 00:14:40,400
Okay, medical history.

303
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:42,400
"Have you ever been diagnosed with diabetes?"

304
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:44,000
- No. - Kidney disease?

305
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:45,730
- No. - Migraines?

306
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:47,800
Getting one.

307
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:50,400
- Are you currently pregnant? - No.

308
00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:52,900
Are you sure? You look a bit puffy.

309
00:14:55,030 --> 00:14:57,130
Change migraine to yes.

310
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:00,360
"When was your last menstrual period?"

311
00:15:00,430 --> 00:15:02,000
Oh. Next question.

312
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:04,130
I'll put, "In progress."

313
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:08,530
Okay, moving to psychiatric disorders.

314
00:15:08,600 --> 00:15:10,360
"List all major behavioral diagnoses,

315
00:15:10,430 --> 00:15:12,430
"e.g. depression, anxiety, etcetera."

316
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:14,860
Oh, my God, what the hell does this

317
00:15:14,930 --> 00:15:16,330
have to do with my stupid shoulder?!

318
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:19,630
"Episodes of sub psychotic rage."

319
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:22,560
Ass.

320
00:15:22,630 --> 00:15:24,630
"Possible Tourette's."

321
00:15:26,030 --> 00:15:30,260
All right. "Moles, lesions or other skin conditions."

322
00:15:30,330 --> 00:15:32,860
"Soup tattoo on right buttock."

323
00:15:32,930 --> 00:15:34,260
Hey, Sheldon, Sheldon, look,

324
00:15:34,330 --> 00:15:36,630
I am scared and in a lot of pain.

325
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:39,630
Could you please take a break from being you for

326
00:15:39,700 --> 00:15:41,560
just a minute and try being--

327
00:15:41,630 --> 00:15:44,460
I don't know-- comforting?

328
00:15:44,530 --> 00:15:47,330
I'm sorry.

329
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:50,500
There, there.

330
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:53,760
Everything's going to be fine.

331
00:16:01,900 --> 00:16:03,900
Sheldon's here.

332
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:14,560
Thanks. That's much better.

333
00:16:15,930 --> 00:16:18,200
Oh, I'm so hungry!

334
00:16:18,260 --> 00:16:19,660
Will you shut up?

335
00:16:19,730 --> 00:16:21,860
We're all hungry.

336
00:16:21,930 --> 00:16:23,700
Okay, our objective is

337
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:25,560
the Boy Scout campsite to the east.

338
00:16:25,630 --> 00:16:26,930
Easy target.

339
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,300
Big doughy scout master, couple of Cubs,

340
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:30,030
mostly WeBeLos.

341
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:31,400
What kind of score we looking at?

342
00:16:31,460 --> 00:16:32,600
Hot dogs, Buns, S'mores.

343
00:16:32,660 --> 00:16:34,160
I mean, it's a freaking 7-11.

344
00:16:34,230 --> 00:16:37,430
All right,everyone grab flashlights.

345
00:16:40,230 --> 00:16:42,530
Oh, my God, could it be?

346
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:43,830
Yes!

347
00:16:43,900 --> 00:16:46,730
My mother put an "I love you" brisket in my backpack.

348
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:47,730
Quick, get forks.

349
00:16:47,800 --> 00:16:49,130
You don't need forks.

350
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:50,900
It's so tender, it falls apart in your hands.

351
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:52,960
He's right.

352
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:03,860
I feel like we're forgetting something important.

353
00:17:03,930 --> 00:17:05,930
Me, too. But what?

354
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:12,960
Maybe a Tupperware bowl of roasted potatoes and carrots?

355
00:17:13,030 --> 00:17:15,000
- Yes! - Yes!

356
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,330
Now remember, you were given powerful pain medication

357
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:25,030
and a muscle relaxer,

358
00:17:25,100 --> 00:17:27,700
so, uh, don't operate heavy machinery.

359
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:32,330
Try not to choke on your own drool.

360
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:36,930
Wait.You have to help me get into bed.

361
00:17:40,030 --> 00:17:43,430
Sheldon has to get me into bed.

362
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:48,130
Bet you never thought I'd say that.

363
00:17:48,200 --> 00:17:50,200
Yes.

364
00:17:51,500 --> 00:17:54,460
The charm of your drug-addled candor knows no bounds.

365
00:17:56,100 --> 00:18:00,600
You know, people think you're this weird robot man

366
00:18:00,660 --> 00:18:04,060
who's so annoying all the time, and you totally are.

367
00:18:04,130 --> 00:18:08,530
But then it's like that movie Wall-E at the end.

368
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:10,600
You're so full of love,

369
00:18:10,660 --> 00:18:13,000
and you can save a plant

370
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:16,730
and get fat people out of the floaty chairs.

371
00:18:16,800 --> 00:18:19,460
That's a fairly labored metaphor,

372
00:18:19,530 --> 00:18:21,900
but I appreciate the sentiment behind it.

373
00:18:21,960 --> 00:18:25,130
Sing "Soft Kitty" to me.

374
00:18:28,930 --> 00:18:30,760
"Soft Kitty" is for when you're sick.

375
00:18:30,830 --> 00:18:32,000
You're not sick.

376
00:18:32,060 --> 00:18:35,300
Injured and drugged is a kind of sick.

377
00:18:44,630 --> 00:18:50,600
Soft kitty, warm kitty, little ball of fur...

378
00:18:50,660 --> 00:18:51,900
Wait, wait.

379
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:53,960
Let's sing it as a round.

380
00:18:54,030 --> 00:18:55,300
I'll start.

381
00:18:56,860 --> 00:19:04,100
Soft kitty, warm kitty, little ball of fur...

382
00:19:04,960 --> 00:19:06,430
So, that's where you come in.

383
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:08,830
I'll start over.

384
00:19:08,900 --> 00:19:15,000
Soft kitty, warm kitty, little ball of fur...

385
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,060
I've got all night, Sheldon.

386
00:19:21,130 --> 00:19:25,460
Soft kitty, warm kitty, little ball of fur...

387
00:19:25,530 --> 00:19:28,800
Soft kitty, warm kitty, little ball of fur...

388
00:19:28,860 --> 00:19:30,930
Happy kitty, sleepy kitty

389
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,300
Happy kitty, sleepy kitty

390
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:35,200
Purr, purr, purr

391
00:19:35,260 --> 00:19:39,460
Purr, purr, purr.

392
00:19:45,560 --> 00:19:48,760
...And the next morning when he woke up,

393
00:19:48,830 --> 00:19:52,130
he rolled over and realized...

394
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:57,730
...she was his cousin.

395
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:07,260
That's still not funny.

396
00:20:08,600 --> 00:20:10,530
That's still not funny.

397
00:20:12,060 --> 00:20:15,000
And she was my second cousin.

398
00:20:15,060 --> 00:20:16,630
And she was my second cousin.

399
00:20:20,460 --> 00:20:22,200
You're a real douche.

400
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:24,660
Who cares? You slept with your cousin.

